{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["<b>I, YHWH, have spoken; if not, etc.</b> One must know what is the intent of the statement <b>I, YHWH, have spoken</b> — do we not know that YHWH is the One who speaks? Further, what is the intent of His saying <b>if not this</b>, etc., and what is the meaning of His saying <b>and there they shall die</b>? Indeed, it intends this: ״I, YHWH״ — which is the attribute of kindness and compassion, she too agreed to the written judgment, and that is His saying <b>I have spoken</b> — I have spoken these harsh words. And if you say, how could this attribute agree to the adversity for Israel? Therefore He said, ״if not this, I shall do״ — and I shall bear long with them to do them good; I know their ultimate end, that they would perish from this world and from the world to come through their provocations, as they would continue to sin, and that is His saying <b>in this wilderness they shall be consumed</b> — meaning, all of them would be utterly destroyed entirely, and to the world to come they would die. Therefore YHWH decreed this judgment, that they would die in the wilderness and not be consumed, for their children He established in their stead, and also to the world to come they would not die, as he who says (Sanhedrin 108a) that they have a portion in the world to come. And from now on, He is compassionate in this judgment."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:09:30.729460079Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}